####
# ======================================================================
# fledge - Ship a Ruby app to people who don't have Ruby
# ======================================================================
#
# SYNOPSIS
#     fledge build [OPTIONS] [PATH]
#     fledge build --from APP [OPTIONS] [PATH]
#     fledge build ios|android [OPTIONS] [PATH]
#     fledge --help
#     fledge --version
#
# DESCRIPTION
#     Put a Ruby app, and a Ruby to run it, in one folder that runs on
#     a computer with no Ruby installed.
#
#     The Ruby on your computer can't travel. It was built for one
#     machine, with that machine's paths and libraries baked in, so a
#     copy breaks anywhere else. Fledge starts instead from a runtime:
#     a Ruby built to be moved, and a small launcher that runs your
#     app on it. Fledge copies your app into the runtime for the
#     computer it runs on, and makes it what that system expects, with
#     that system's own tools; build on a Mac for macOS, on Windows for
#     Windows, and on Linux for Linux, as a CI runner of each does.
#
#     Build the program you already run, such as exe/my_app in a gem,
#     bin/server in an app with a Gemfile, or a lone main.rb. Its
#     project ships with it, and so do the gems its Gemfile.lock locks,
#     each as built for this platform, which Bundler sets up before the
#     program runs, as bundle exec would. A gem with an extension to
#     compile (published only as source, or from git) is compiled
#     against the runtime's own Ruby, with your compiler.
#
#     The app's Ruby is the one you run fledge build with: the runtime
#     has that Ruby's major and minor version, such as 4.0, at the
#     patch level this Fledge ships, and the build fails when this
#     Fledge has no runtime for it. A ruby line in the Gemfile must
#     accept the runtime's Ruby. Your code runs as it does under ruby
#     main.rb or bundle exec; doc/differences_from_ruby.md lists each
#     difference, and why.
#
#     An app is console (it runs in a terminal) or windowed (it opens
#     its own windows): a windowed app is an .app bundle on macOS,
#     signed ad hoc, a program Windows starts without a console, and a
#     folder with a desktop entry and icons on Linux. Every app carries
#     the name, version, publisher, icon, description, and homepage
#     fledge.yml gives, where each system shows them, on Windows in the
#     program itself. A
#     windowed app gets an installer beside each archive, too: a disk
#     image for macOS, a setup program for Windows, which makes the
#     Start menu shortcut Windows needs before it shows the app's
#     notifications, and an AppImage for Linux, and, when fledge.yml
#     asks for it, a .deb for Debian and Ubuntu.
#
#     An app Fledge built already holds a runtime and its gems, each
#     built for its platform, so a project that needs no other gems
#     can be built from it, on a computer of the same platform with
#     nothing else: fledge build --from copies the app, puts the
#     project in place of its own, writes the new app's name and
#     identity with the system's own tools, and archives it, and
#     downloads, installs, and compiles nothing. On Windows it takes
#     the program, rcedit, and NSIS from APP's tools folder, which an
#     app built with build_tools: true carries, or else from this
#     Fledge's runtime, which must then be on the computer; on Linux
#     an AppImage needs the runtime's AppImage runtime the same way.
#
#     For a phone, fledge build writes the project that builds the
#     app, which you build, sign, and submit with that system's tools:
#     an Xcode project for iPhone and iPad on a Mac, and a Gradle project
#     for Android on a Mac, Linux, or Windows. Ruby runs there as a
#     library linked into the app, with Bundler setting up the app's
#     gems; a gem with a C or C++ extension (an extconf.rb) is compiled
#     for each of the phone's targets with Xcode or the Android NDK and
#     linked into the app; what the app prints goes to the system log.
#
#     An app is unsigned unless you sign it with your own identity
#     (--sign), so that macOS's Gatekeeper and Windows' SmartScreen
#     can trust it: on macOS every Mach-O file, the bundle, and the
#     disk image, with the hardened runtime and a timestamp, by
#     codesign; on Windows every program and library, and the setup
#     program and its uninstaller, by signtool from the Windows SDK,
#     or by a signing command of your own (--sign-command), for a
#     certificate kept in a cloud or hardware service. On macOS,
#     --notarize then has Apple's notary service notarize the signed
#     app, with notarytool and the credentials you stored in your
#     keychain once, and staples Apple's ticket to the disk image and
#     the bundle before the archive is packed.
#
#     Each system's own tools do the work. macOS has them all (tar,
#     plutil, codesign, ditto, hdiutil). Windows 10 and 11 have tar;
#     the runtime brings rcedit, for a program's version information
#     and icon, and NSIS, for a setup program. Linux has tar, and an
#     AppImage needs squashfs-tools' mksquashfs (4.4 or newer), and a
#     .deb needs dpkg-deb, from dpkg, and, when fledge.yml lists system
#     libraries, dpkg-shlibdeps, from dpkg-dev. A missing tool fails
#     the build, naming it and what to install.
#
# COMMANDS
#     build              Copy the program PATH (default: the current
#                        directory) and its project into this
#                        computer's runtime, with the gems it needs,
#                        then archive the result, and for a windowed
#                        app write its installer, each with its
#                        checksum file. Prints the path of the archive
#                        and installer it writes, one per line.
#                        A folder means the one executable its gemspec
#                        names, or else its main.rb; when fledge.yml
#                        lists programs, every one of them, and a file
#                        it lists means that program alone.
#     build --from APP   Build the program PATH as build does, but with
#                        the runtime and gems of APP, the folder of an
#                        app this Fledge built for this computer (on
#                        macOS, its .app too), in place of the
#                        runtime's and the Gemfile.lock's. The new app
#                        runs PATH's project with APP's Gemfile and
#                        Gemfile.lock, and its own name and identity;
#                        nothing of APP's project or identity stays.
#                        PATH's project may have no Gemfile, gems.rb,
#                        or gemspec, nor a file where APP keeps one
#                        Bundler needs, and its fledge.yml no programs,
#                        linux: system_libraries, ios, or android,
#                        which change the runtime or the gems. An APP
#                        another Fledge
#                        release built, or built for another system,
#                        is a usage error.
#     build ios          Write an Xcode project for the app, for iPhone
#                        and iPad (iOS 15 and later, arm64), which
#                        builds for devices and the Simulator, and print
#                        its path. Run it on a Mac, with Xcode and
#                        XcodeGen (brew install xcodegen), which writes
#                        the project.
#     build android      Write a Gradle project for the app, for Android
#                        8.0 (API 26) and later, targeting API 36, with
#                        Gradle's wrapper (./gradlew assembleDebug), and
#                        print its path. Run it on a Mac, x86-64 Linux,
#                        or Windows; building the project needs a JDK and
#                        the Android SDK.
#                        A phone's name comes first. A PATH that is a
#                        bare word Fledge reads as a platform, today
#                        ios and android, is read as one, and so is
#                        each platform's word a later release adds;
#                        write ./NAME for a folder of that name.
#
# OPTIONS
#     -o, --out DIR      Write builds to DIR (default: dist)
#     --from APP         Build from the app in the folder APP (see
#                        build --from); not for a phone
#     --sign IDENTITY    Sign the app with your own identity: on
#                        macOS a code signing identity in your
#                        keychain, as codesign names it, such as
#                        "Developer ID Application: NAME (TEAM)", or
#                        - for ad hoc; on Windows the SHA-1 thumbprint
#                        of a code signing certificate in your
#                        certificate store. Not on Linux, or for a
#                        phone, which Xcode and Gradle sign.
#     --sign-command COMMAND
#                        Sign a Windows app with your own command, in
#                        place of --sign: Fledge runs COMMAND, through
#                        cmd.exe, once for each program and library,
#                        then the uninstaller and the setup program,
#                        with the file's path, quoted, in place of %1,
#                        or after COMMAND when it has no %1. A COMMAND
#                        that exits with anything but 0 fails the
#                        build, naming the file. Windows only.
#     --notarize PROFILE Have Apple notarize the macOS app --sign
#                        signed with a Developer ID Application
#                        identity, with the credentials xcrun
#                        notarytool store-credentials PROFILE stored in
#                        your keychain, and staple Apple's ticket to the
#                        disk image and the bundle. A console app's
#                        folder is notarized in a zip archive, and
#                        Gatekeeper finds its ticket online. Waits for
#                        Apple, usually minutes. macOS only.
#     -h, -?, --help     Show this help
#     -v, --version      Print Fledge's version
#
# FILES
#     ROOT               The program's project: the nearest folder,
#                        from the program's up, with a Gemfile,
#                        gems.rb, a gemspec, or a fledge.yml, but not
#                        above a git repository's top; else the
#                        program's own folder. Everything in it ships,
#                        as app/, except .git, .bundle, vendor/bundle,
#                        vendor/cache, DIR, and every folder that holds
#                        a CACHEDIR.TAG, as each folder a build went to
#                        does.
#     ROOT/Gemfile.lock  The gems to ship, without the development and
#                        test groups. It must lock this computer's
#                        platform (bundle lock --add-platform), each
#                        gem's checksum (bundle lock --add-checksums),
#                        and each git gem's commit. Every gem ships
#                        with its own files and notices, whatever its
#                        license, which is yours to comply with. A gem
#                        that does not support the runtime's Ruby, or
#                        whose compiled files need a library another
#                        computer may lack, fails the build, unless
#                        fledge.yml lists it as the Linux
#                        distribution's. A gem with
#                        an extension to compile is compiled with
#                        bundle config build.NAME's options: Xcode's
#                        command line tools on macOS, a C compiler and
#                        make on Linux, RubyInstaller's MSYS2 UCRT64
#                        tools (ridk install) on Windows, and cargo for
#                        a Rust extension.
#     ROOT/fledge.yml    Optional, a YAML mapping of:
#                        name: the app's name (default: the gemspec's
#                          name, or ROOT's folder name, camelized)
#                        kind: console (default) or windowed
#                        identifier: the app's reverse-DNS name, such as
#                          com.example.MyCoolApp, which macOS and
#                          Windows know it by, and the bundle identifier
#                          or applicationId of a phone's app (needed when
#                          windowed, and for a phone). Changing
#                          identifier makes a new app to each system,
#                          so Windows and apt register it beside the
#                          old one.
#                        version: such as "1.2.3" (default: the
#                          gemspec's, or 0.0.0)
#                        icon: the icon's files without their
#                          extension, as Briefcase names them, such
#                          as icons/app: icons/app.icns on macOS,
#                          icons/app.ico on Windows (16 to 256
#                          pixels), icons/app-16.png, -32, -64, -128,
#                          -256, and -512 on Linux (those there are,
#                          at least one; the build names the rest),
#                          icons/app-1024.png for iOS, and
#                          icons/app-48.png, -72, -96, -144, and -192
#                          for Android
#                        publisher: who makes it (default: the
#                          gemspec's authors)
#                        copyright: such as "© 2026 Example, Inc."
#                        description: one line that says what the app
#                          does, such as Keeps your notes: a desktop
#                          entry's Comment, a .deb's synopsis, and a
#                          Windows program's Comments (default: none)
#                        long_description: more about the app, in
#                          paragraphs: a .deb's extended description
#                        homepage: the app's web page, an http or https
#                          URL: a .deb's Homepage, and the links of the
#                          setup program's entry in Windows' Settings
#                        license_file: the app's license text, a UTF-8
#                          file in ROOT, such as LICENSE.txt, which the
#                          setup program shows on a page before it
#                          installs, and a .deb installs as
#                          /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE/copyright
#                        installers: which of dmg, nsis, appimage, and
#                          deb a windowed app gets, such as [dmg] or []
#                          for none (default: dmg, nsis, and appimage)
#                        programs: the programs to ship in one folder,
#                          sharing one runtime, project, and set of
#                          gems, each a mapping of path (its file, such
#                          as bin/cli), name (default: the file's name
#                          without its extension for a console program,
#                          and the app's name for a windowed one), kind,
#                          identifier, and icon; at most one is
#                          windowed, and gets the installers. Beside
#                          programs, kind, identifier, and icon belong
#                          to each program.
#                        ios, android: a mapping of host, a folder in
#                          ROOT, such as host/ios, whose files build ios
#                          or build android copies over the project it
#                          writes, before XcodeGen runs: each replaces
#                          Fledge's file at its path, or joins them, and
#                          the build says which it replaced. Desktop
#                          builds ignore them.
#                        linux: a mapping of system_libraries, the
#                          libraries the gems load from the Linux
#                          distribution, by the names their compiled
#                          files give them, such as
#                          [libwebkit2gtk-4.1.so.0, libgtk-3.so.0]. The
#                          gems may need them, and the build fails on
#                          one that none needs; a .deb depends on their
#                          packages; the app names one a computer
#                          lacks, and exits with 72, before Ruby starts.
#                          It may also hold deb_changelog, the app's
#                          changelog, a file in ROOT, which a .deb
#                          installs as /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE/
#                          changelog.gz, compressed with gzip -n -9.
#                          Other builds ignore it.
#                        macos: a mapping of build, the build number
#                          a windowed app's Info.plist gives as
#                          CFBundleVersion, which the App Store and
#                          updaters compare: one to three whole numbers
#                          between periods, quoted, such as "42"
#                          (default: the version's first three
#                          numbers). Other builds ignore it.
#                        file_associations: what a windowed app opens,
#                          a list of mappings of name (what a person
#                          calls the documents), role (viewer, the
#                          default, or editor), and extensions (without
#                          the dot), mime_types, and content_types
#                          (macOS's uniform types), or folders: true,
#                          in an entry of its own. macOS's Info.plist
#                          declares them, ranked Alternate, so the app
#                          is offered and never made their default; the
#                          setup program adds the app to each
#                          extension's "Open with" and to every
#                          folder's menu, under HKCU, and a portable
#                          folder registers nothing; the desktop entry
#                          lists the MIME types, inode/directory for
#                          folders, and takes their paths (%F). A file
#                          or folder dropped on a Windows program, or
#                          opened with a Linux one, arrives in ARGV; on
#                          macOS it arrives as an Apple event, which only
#                          a GUI library's event loop receives, and ARGV
#                          stays empty, as under ruby main.rb.
#                        build_tools: true to carry, in the app's tools
#                          folder, what fledge build --from needs that
#                          a system lacks, so that an app with Fledge
#                          in its Gemfile builds from itself where no
#                          runtime is: on Windows the runtime's
#                          launchers, rcedit, and NSIS, on Linux the
#                          AppImage runtime, and on macOS nothing
#                          (default: false). An app built --from
#                          carries them only if it sets build_tools too.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM/ The built app, ready to run where it lands.
#                        PLATFORM is this computer's: arm64-darwin,
#                        x86_64-linux, aarch64-linux,
#                        x86_64-linux-musl, aarch64-linux-musl,
#                        x64-mingw-ucrt, aarch64-mingw-ucrt. A -linux
#                        app runs on Linux with glibc 2.39 or newer, a
#                        -linux-musl one on Linux with musl, such as
#                        Alpine, and aarch64-mingw-ucrt on Windows 11
#                        on ARM64.
#                        NAME is the gemspec's name, or else ROOT's
#                        folder name, camelized (my_cool_app is
#                        MyCoolApp), unless fledge.yml names it. NAME
#                        is also the program to run (NAME.exe on
#                        Windows), or, for a windowed app on macOS,
#                        the bundle NAME.app; a windowed Linux app has
#                        IDENTIFIER.desktop and share/icons/ beside
#                        it. It runs app/PROGRAM as ruby, or bundle
#                        exec, would, and exits with its status, or
#                        with 66 when it is missing, 72 on Linux when
#                        a library fledge.yml lists is not on the
#                        computer, and 70 when the rest of the folder
#                        is broken. On macOS and
#                        Linux, exec RbConfig.ruby, $0, *ARGV
#                        restarts it the same way; on Windows, start
#                        NAME.exe again. Its notices/
#                        holds the licenses of what Fledge put in it,
#                        and on Linux and Windows, in sources/, the
#                        source of the LGPL libraries and the
#                        installer tools whose licenses ask for it;
#                        each gem keeps its own in its folder. A new
#                        build replaces it.
#                        With programs, each is a program of its own
#                        name beside the one runtime and app/, but on
#                        macOS with a windowed program, whose bundle
#                        holds the rest in Contents/MacOS, linked from
#                        the folder. A program renamed by hand exits
#                        with 66.
#     DIR/NAME-ios/      An iOS app's Xcode project: NAME.xcodeproj,
#                        which XcodeGen wrote from project.yml, beside
#                        it; FledgeRuby.xcframework, the runtime, whose
#                        Ruby for the SDK built for a build phase copies
#                        into the app; fledge/, with fledge.cfg, the
#                        project in app/, the app's gems, and notices/;
#                        and Host/, the app's main.m, Info.plist, privacy
#                        manifest, and icon. No signing is configured.
#     DIR/NAME-android/  An Android app's Gradle project: build.gradle,
#                        with the app's identity; libs/FledgeRuby.aar,
#                        the runtime; fledge/, with the runtime's Ruby,
#                        fledge.cfg, the project in app/, the app's gems,
#                        and notices/, which the build stores as the
#                        asset fledge.zip; and src/main/, the app's
#                        MainActivity.java and icons. A debug build is
#                        signed with the SDK's debug key, and a release
#                        build left unsigned.
#                        A phone's app sets up its gems with Bundler's
#                        force_ruby_platform, so its Gemfile.lock must
#                        lock ruby (bundle lock --add-platform ruby). A
#                        gem with a C or C++ extension is compiled for
#                        the phone and linked into the app (Android needs
#                        the NDK: ANDROID_NDK_ROOT or the SDK's ndk/
#                        folder). An extconf.rb that runs cmake finds
#                        each target's toolchain file in
#                        CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE. A gem whose extension is
#                        not built by an extconf.rb, or is Rust, fails the
#                        build, naming it.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.zip
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.tar.gz
#                        The same folder, archived for download: a .zip
#                        for Windows, a .tar.gz for macOS and Linux.
#     DIR/NAME-arm64-darwin.dmg
#                        A windowed app's disk image: NAME.app and a
#                        link to Applications, to drag it onto.
#     DIR/NAME-x64-mingw-ucrt-setup.exe
#                        A windowed app's NSIS installer, which installs
#                        for the person who runs it, with no
#                        administrator, into %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs,
#                        makes a Start menu shortcut whose
#                        AppUserModelID is the identifier, offers a
#                        desktop shortcut, and registers an uninstaller.
#                        With a license_file, it shows the license
#                        first, for the person to accept. /S installs
#                        silently, with no page at all.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.AppImage
#                        A windowed app's AppImage for Linux, which runs
#                        from its own FUSE mount, as every AppImage
#                        does; on a computer with no FUSE,
#                        APPIMAGE_EXTRACT_AND_RUN=1 has it extract
#                        itself and run.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.deb
#                        A windowed app's .deb for x86_64-linux and
#                        aarch64-linux, when fledge.yml lists deb:
#                        apt install ./FILE puts the app in
#                        /usr/lib/IDENTIFIER, with its desktop entry
#                        and icons where desktops look, and its
#                        license_file and deb_changelog in
#                        /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE, and needs
#                        glibc 2.39 or newer; apt remove removes it.
#                        It depends on libc6, and on the packages that
#                        provide the libraries linux: system_libraries
#                        lists, as dpkg-shlibdeps finds them for the
#                        files that need them. A library a gem opens at
#                        run time, such as the X11, Wayland, or OpenGL
#                        that SDL opens, is the desktop's, and apt does
#                        not check for it.
#     DIR/FILE.sha256    Beside the archive and each installer, its
#                        SHA-256, as sha256sum prints it: the hash, two
#                        spaces, and FILE's name. In DIR,
#                        sha256sum -c FILE.sha256 (shasum -a 256 -c on
#                        macOS) checks FILE; a package manager's recipe
#                        names the hash. Not printed.
#     DIR/CACHEDIR.TAG   A cache directory tag (bford.info/cachedir),
#                        written when the build makes DIR or finds it
#                        empty, which keeps DIR out of every later
#                        build's app/, and out of backups that honor it.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/runtimes/
#                        Runtimes downloaded from Fledge's GitHub
#                        Release for this version (or from
#                        FLEDGE_RUNTIME_MIRROR), each checked
#                        against the SHA-256 this Fledge pins for it,
#                        in the cache too, and, to
#                        compile gems, each one's build-only companion
#                        of Ruby's headers and libraries. A runtime's
#                        tools/ (rcedit and NSIS on Windows, AppImage's
#                        runtime on Linux) never ships in an app.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/gems/
#                        Gems downloaded from the lockfile's gem
#                        server, each checked against the lockfile's
#                        checksum. ROOT/vendor/cache and RubyGems' own
#                        caches are looked in first.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/git/
#                        Repositories of the lockfile's git gems, which
#                        ship at the commit it locks.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#     FLEDGE_RUNTIME_DIR Take runtimes from this folder of
#                        fledge-runtime-VERSION-PLATFORM.tar.gz files
#                        (and their -dev.tar.gz companions), and never
#                        download them.
#     FLEDGE_RUNTIME_MIRROR
#                        Download runtimes from this server instead
#                        of Fledge's GitHub Release, as
#                        MIRROR/vVERSION/NAME, the release's own
#                        paths. Every file is checked against the
#                        SHA-256 this Fledge pins, and a file the
#                        mirror lacks fails the build.
#     FLEDGE_SIGN_COMMAND
#                        On Windows, the COMMAND of --sign-command for
#                        a build whose command line gives neither
#                        --sign nor --sign-command. Other systems'
#                        builds ignore it.
#     XDG_CACHE_HOME     Keep downloaded runtimes and gems in
#                        $XDG_CACHE_HOME/fledge instead.
#
# EXIT STATUS
#     0                  Success
#     1                  The build failed (a missing app, a computer
#                        Fledge has no runtime for, or that does not
#                        build the phone's project, a runtime that
#                        would not download, a gem that cannot ship or
#                        would not compile, a missing tool or icon
#                        file, an identity or a signing command that
#                        would not sign, an app Apple would not
#                        notarize, a full disk)
#     2                  Usage error (an unrecognized option, a
#                        missing value)
#
# EXAMPLES
#     Build the gem in the current directory for this computer:
#     fledge build
#
#     Build a gem's executable, here and on a computer of each other
#     platform, such as a CI runner of each:
#     fledge build exe/my_cool_app
#
#     Write the app's Xcode project on a Mac, and its Gradle project:
#     fledge build ios
#     fledge build android
#
#     Build and sign with your Developer ID, and notarize, on a Mac:
#     fledge build --sign "Developer ID Application: Example (ABCDE12345)" \
#       --notarize my-notary-profile
#
#     Build and sign with Azure Artifact Signing, on Windows:
#     fledge build --sign-command "signtool sign /fd sha256 /tr
#       http://timestamp.acs.microsoft.com /td sha256 /dlib
#       C:\ArtifactSigning\bin\x64\Azure.CodeSigning.Dlib.dll /dmdf
#       C:\ArtifactSigning\metadata.json %1"
#
#     Build a script from elsewhere, into another folder:
#     fledge build --out /tmp/builds path/to/app/main.rb
#
# ======================================================================
###

require "fledge/cli"
exit Fledge::CLI.new(help_path: __FILE__).call(ARGV)
